      "baseline": "Solve the user's problem by constructing one arithmetic expression. A separate calculator will evaluate your expression; do not manually evaluate it. Use the original given values, any conditioning information, and the requested units. Use standard unit-conversion constants when needed. Return ONLY one valid JSON object in this structure: {\"explanation\":\"Briefly explain the setup.\",\"expression\":\"Unevaluated arithmetic expression\"}. Both fields must be strings. Keep explanation to at most 60 words. The expression may contain only plain integer or decimal numbers, parentheses, and +, -, *, /, including unary signs. Do not use names, functions, powers, LaTeX, Markdown, or text outside the JSON object.",
      "syntax_clear": "Solve the user's problem by constructing one arithmetic expression. A separate calculator will evaluate your expression; do not manually evaluate it. Use the original given values, any conditioning information, and the requested units. Use standard unit-conversion constants when needed. Return ONLY one valid JSON object in this structure: {\"explanation\":\"Briefly explain the setup.\",\"expression\":\"Unevaluated arithmetic expression\"}. Both fields must be strings. Keep explanation to at most 60 words. The expression may contain only plain integer or decimal numbers, parentheses, and +, -, *, /, including unary signs. Do not use names, functions, powers, LaTeX, Markdown, or text outside the JSON object. Expression-format contract: expression must be one NUMERIC expression, not an equation or an intermediate symbolic rearrangement. For algebra, finish isolating the requested unknown in your setup, then put only a numeric right-hand-side expression for its value in expression. Never put variable names or an equals sign in expression. Use only ASCII digits 0-9, decimal points, spaces, parentheses, and the operators +, -, *, /. Write ASCII '*' for EVERY multiplication and '/' for division. Do not use Unicode operators, implicit multiplication, scientific notation, functions, units, or prose in expression. These are syntax examples only, unrelated to your question. VALID: '(2+3)*4'; '2*(3+4)'; '(7-1)/(5+2)'. INVALID: 'x = (2+3)*4'; '3*y+5'; '2\u00d73'; '2\u22173'; '2(3+4)'. Before sending, check that expression has balanced parentheses, no letters or '=', and an explicit ASCII operator between multiplied quantities. Return the original two-field JSON object; do not manually evaluate the expression."
